    "case_id": "743",
    "case_summary": "A 56-year-old male presented with right lower abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting for one month. The vomitus included stomach contents and bile. He had undergone bariatric surgery 10 years prior. His weight history showed: preoperative weight 127 kg, decreased to 95 kg three months post-surgery, increased to 118 kg five months ago due to changed eating habits, and recently lost 23 kg due to poor appetite and abdominal pain.\n\nPhysical findings included:- Localized tenderness and rebound pain in the right lower abdomen- No obvious muscle tension- Active bowel sounds- Sound of gas passing through water could be heard- Vital signs were stable\n\nLaboratory Tests:- White blood cells: 15.3 × 109- Neutrophils: 13.25 × 109- C-reactive protein: 190 mg/L- Albumin: 24 g/L- Normal renal function and coagulation testsImaging Studies:1. Plain Abdominal Radiography:- Free gas under the right diaphragm- Dilated upper jejunum with 'spring sign'2. Abdominal CT:- Scattered pneumatosis in abdominal cavity- Thickened intestinal wall- Dense tubular shadows in narrow lumen3. Upper Digestive Tract Examination:Title: Upper gastrointestinal radiographyDescription: Shows constriction of the junction of gastric fundus and body, increased annular density shadow in front of the ilium in right lower abdomen, and dilation of the duodenum and proximal jejunum",
